## Module Re-exports (`pub use`)

### The Problem

```xiom
mod utils {
  pub mod parser {
    pub fn parse(input: Str) -> Int { 0 }
  }
  pub mod formatter {
    pub fn format(n: Int) -> Str { "0" }
  }
}
// Users must write: utils.parser.parse("42")
```

### The Solution: Re-export

```xiom
mod utils {
  pub mod parser {
    pub fn parse(input: Str) -> Int { 0 }
  }
  pub mod formatter {
    pub fn format(n: Int) -> Str { "0" }
  }

  pub use parser::parse;
  pub use formatter::format;
}

// Now users write: utils.parse("42")
```

`pub use inner_module::function;` brings the function up to the parent module AND makes it public there. Users get a clean interface. You keep organized internals.

### Renaming Exports

```xiom
pub use parser::parse as parse_int;
```

This exports `parse` under a different name `parse_int`. Useful for avoiding name collisions.

